+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Build the replacement index into a fresh, separate database file so partially imported records are never visible. Activate only after validation and index construction both succeed. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
 
-## Lookup Behavior
+## Index and Lookup
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+The active snapshot is a single SQLite database file whose records are keyed by normalized serial number. Import normalizes each record's serial number, inserts the record under that key, stores the snapshot date, and closes the file before activation.
+
+Lookup normalizes the requested serial number the same way, then queries the active file. Normalization is identical at build and lookup time: surrounding whitespace is removed and ASCII letters are uppercased; punctuation is preserved. Return the matching record with the snapshot date, or `NotFound` with the snapshot date when no record matches.
+
+## Activation
+
+Activation swaps the active index to the newly built file; the previous snapshot stays available until the swap succeeds. A reader sees either the previous snapshot or the new one in full, never a mixture.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Keeps every lookup on the kiosk, honoring the no-network tenet; no service to operate; index build stays inside this node. Accepts per-kiosk revalidation and rebuild on each snapshot. |
